*OpenUI *Resolution/Print Resolution: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *Resolution
*DefaultResolution: 600dpi
*Resolution 300dpi/300 DPI (Fast): "<</HWResolution[300 300]>>setpagedevice"
*Resolution 600dpi/600 DPI (Best): "<</HWResolution[600 600]>>setpagedevice"
*CloseUI: *Resolution

*% All three modes take the same 8-bit RGB raster from CUPS; they differ in
*% what the driver does with it. KCMY separates to black/cyan/magenta/yellow
*% here, with full grey component replacement, dithers each channel to one
*% bit and sends four planes through the Simple Colour KCMY palette - so
*% black prints with black ink instead of being mixed from CMY, and only
*% 4 bits per pixel go over the wire. RGB hands 24-bit colour to the printer
*% and lets it decide how to lay ink down, which is what the driver used to
*% do; keep it as a fallback. Gray sends a single black plane.
*OpenUI *ColorModel/Color Mode: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *ColorModel
*DefaultColorModel: RGB
*ColorModel KCMY/Color - separated in driver: "<</cupsColorSpace 1/cupsColorOrder 0/cupsBitsPerColor 8>>setpagedevice"
*ColorModel RGB/Color - printer screens (recommended): "<</cupsColorSpace 1/cupsColorOrder 0/cupsBitsPerColor 8>>setpagedevice"
*ColorModel Gray/Black ink only: "<</cupsColorSpace 1/cupsColorOrder 0/cupsBitsPerColor 8>>setpagedevice"
*CloseUI: *ColorModel

*% Which halftone the *printer* uses, sent as ESC*t#J. Only has an effect
*% in RGB colour mode, where the device is the one screening the image; the
*% separated modes have already reduced everything to one bit. HP's own
*% driver sends 13 (scatter dither). Printer leaves the device on its own
*% default, which is what this driver did historically.
*OpenUI *RenderAlgorithm/Printer Halftone: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *RenderAlgorithm
*DefaultRenderAlgorithm: 13
*RenderAlgorithm Printer/Device default (bands on flat tone): ""
*RenderAlgorithm 0/Device best: ""
*RenderAlgorithm 7/Cluster-ordered dither: ""
*RenderAlgorithm 11/Pattern dither: ""
*RenderAlgorithm 13/Scatter dither (HP driver uses this): ""
*CloseUI: *RenderAlgorithm

*% Print quality, sent as the HP-GL/2 QL instruction in the picture header.
*% Note this is HP-GL/2, not PJL - this device exposes no PJL quality
*% variables at all, which is what makes it look at first as though quality
*% cannot be driven from the host. Per the spec a device "might vary paper
*% speed, resolution, or rasterization algorithms" in response, i.e. this is
*% the control over how many passes the head makes, and so over how much
*% banding shows. HP's own Windows driver sends QL51.
*% Printer means send nothing and leave the front panel in charge, which is
*% the historical behaviour of this driver.
*OpenUI *QualityLevel/Print Quality: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *QualityLevel
*DefaultQualityLevel: Printer
*QualityLevel Printer/Use printer front panel: ""
*QualityLevel Draft/Draft - fastest, least ink: ""
*QualityLevel Normal/Normal: ""
*QualityLevel Best/Best - most passes, least banding: ""
*CloseUI: *QualityLevel

*% Whether to hand the device the image size and the area it should cover
*% and let it resample (ESC*r3A), rather than placing our pixels one for
*% one (ESC*r1A). Scaling lets the firmware own the mapping from our rows
*% onto its own dot rows, so it can line that up with the swaths it is
*% about to lay down; a 1:1 placement gives it no such freedom and shows as
*% banding across smooth gradients. Confirmed on the hardware - the same
*% photograph bands unscaled and is clean scaled. HP's driver always
*% scales. Ignored outside RGB, since the spec drops scaling under a
*% Simple Colour palette.
*OpenUI *ScaledRaster/Let Printer Scale Raster: Boolean
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *ScaledRaster
*DefaultScaledRaster: True
*ScaledRaster True/On - printer resamples (recommended): ""
*ScaledRaster False/Off - send pixels one for one: ""
*CloseUI: *ScaledRaster

*% Whether to tell the device it may print as data arrives (ESC&a1N).
*% Doing so bounds its memory use, but forces it to commit each swath
*% immediately instead of arranging passes over the whole page - HP's own
*% driver never sends it. Off by default; turn on only if a very large job
*% exhausts the device.
*OpenUI *OnTheFly/Print While Receiving: Boolean
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *OnTheFly
*DefaultOnTheFly: False
*OnTheFly False/Off - let the printer compose the page: ""
*OnTheFly True/On - print as data arrives: ""
*CloseUI: *OnTheFly

*% Whether the page is split into several Start/End Raster Graphics blocks.
*% Off - one block for the whole page - is both the default and the form
*% HP's own examples use. Splitting was tried as a defence against the
*% device running out of memory, but it introduced visible seams across
*% flat tone at exactly one band's spacing, because End Raster Graphics
*% moves the CAP and zero-fills the area it moves through. Memory is
*% already handled by the negative-motion-off command the driver sends,
*% which lets the device print as data arrives. Keep this Off unless a very
*% large job loses its bottom edge, in which case try 8 MB.
*OpenUI *Banding/Raster Banding: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *Banding
*DefaultBanding: Off
*Banding Off/Off - one block per page (recommended): ""
*Banding 8MB/8 MB blocks: ""
*Banding 2MB/2 MB blocks: ""
*CloseUI: *Banding

*% How much of a neutral is printed with black ink rather than mixed from
*% cyan, magenta and yellow. Full sends every neutral to black, which is
*% ideal for text and line work but can flatten photographic shadows.
*% Backing it off raises the tone at which black *starts* instead of
*% scaling black down, so pure black still prints as pure black ink at
*% every setting - otherwise softening this for photographs would put
*% coloured ink back underneath black text. Applies to KCMY only: RGB
*% leaves separation to the printer, and Gray has a single black plane with
*% no chromatic channels to move a grey component out of.
*OpenUI *BlackGeneration/Black Generation: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *BlackGeneration
*DefaultBlackGeneration: Full
*BlackGeneration Full/Full - neutrals are black ink (line work): ""
*BlackGeneration Heavy/Heavy - mostly black: ""
*BlackGeneration Medium/Medium - richer shadows: ""
*BlackGeneration Light/Light - black only in deep shadows (photos): ""
*CloseUI: *BlackGeneration

*% How the separated channels are reduced to the one bit per pixel the
*% printer's KCMY palette accepts. Ordered is the default because its
*% threshold pattern repeats every 8 pixels - exactly one packed byte - so
*% flat areas compress to almost nothing; error diffusion turns the same
*% areas into incompressible noise and made a full-page gradient 7x larger
*% in measurement. Diffusion is smoother on photographs. Ignored when Color
*% Mode is RGB, since then the printer does its own halftoning.
*OpenUI *Dither/Halftone Method: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *Dither
*DefaultDither: Ordered
*Dither Ordered/Ordered - smaller jobs (recommended): ""
*Dither Diffusion/Error diffusion - smoother photos: ""
*CloseUI: *Dither

*% Driver-side gamma correction, applied by rastertohpgl2rtl as a 256-entry
*% lookup table using the transfer function the HP-GL/2 and HP RTL Reference
*% Guide gives for this printer family: new = (old/255)^(1/gamma) * 255.
*% Higher gamma lightens the page and uses less ink; lower gamma darkens it
*% and uses more. The guide notes ~2.5 is typical for a DesignJet on HP
*% Special Paper. Keywords are "g" + gamma*100 because a bare "2.5" is not a
*% legal PPD option keyword.
*OpenUI *Gamma/Ink Density (Gamma): PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *Gamma
*DefaultGamma: g100
*Gamma g060/0.6 - Much more ink (darkest): ""
*Gamma g080/0.8 - More ink (darker): ""
*Gamma g100/1.0 - No correction: ""
*Gamma g140/1.4 - Less ink (lighter): ""
*Gamma g180/1.8 - Much less ink: ""
*Gamma g220/2.2 - sRGB-like: ""
*Gamma g250/2.5 - HP suggested for DesignJet: ""
*Gamma g300/3.0 - Least ink (lightest): ""
*CloseUI: *Gamma

*DefaultFont: Courier
*Font Courier: Standard "(1.0)" Standard ROM
*%End of PPD file.
